    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "right, so it's more about interpreting the output against what we know about the world. \n\nbut it still feels a bit spooky. like, when we talk about these signals having \"hidden\" or \"latent\" layers that we're pulling out with math, it almost feels like we're uncovering some kind of secret, mischievous force that's working behind the scenes of the data. is there any way to frame it that's less about \"finding things that were already hiding there\" and more about just a change in perspective?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It is entirely understandable why it feels spooky; the shift from the familiar time domain to the abstract frequency domain is a big leap! It's less about uncovering a secret, mischievous force and more about changing the *language* you use to describe the signal. Think of it like this: instead of describing a journey by listing every single footstep (the time domain), the frequency domain describes the *rhythm* of the journey—is it a steady march, a frantic sprint, or a slow, meandering stroll? The Fourier Transform is simply translating the description from \"what happens when\" to \"what is the underlying pace.\"",
      "source": "target",
